  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Puccinia sparganioidis is a rust fungus in the Pucciniaceae family that primarily affects ash trees and related species throughout New England. It completes its complex life cycle by alternating between ash hosts and plants in the Sparganium genus (bur-reeds).",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Uredinia and Telia on Ash",
      "description": "Orange to reddish-brown pustules (uredinia) appear on ash leaf undersides in summer, followed by darker, hardened pustules (telia) later in the season",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Aecial Cups on Alternate Host",
      "description": "Cup-shaped aecia with yellow-orange spores develop on Sparganium leaves, the heteroecious alternate host",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaf Distortion",
      "description": "Infected ash leaves may show yellowing, wilting, or premature drop around pustule sites",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Puccinia sparganioidis thrives in moist environments where ash trees grow near wetlands, streams, or other areas where Sparganium (bur-reed) plants are present, typical of New England's temperate climate.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This rust fungus requires two completely different plant hosts to complete its life cycle—a strategy called heteroecism—making it dependent on the proximity of both ash trees and bur-reed plants for survival and reproduction.",
